            <ArticleTitle>Proprioceptive deficit after total elbow arthroplasty: an observational study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2017-2022</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1058-2746(17)30424-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jse.2017.07.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">During total elbow arthroplasty (TEA), most of the joint capsule is removed, including many mechanoreceptors important for proprioception, which potentially limits the patient's postoperative functional recovery. We quantified proprioceptive loss by measuring the threshold to detection of passive motion (TTDPM) in patients after unilateral TEA compared with the contralateral side.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A continuous passive motion device moving the elbow at 0.5°/s was used to evaluate TTDPM in 8 patients (mean ± standard deviation age, 69.1 ± 9.93 years) at least 1 year after unilateral semiconstricted linked TEA for a range of diagnoses. Elbow function after TEA was assessed using the Mayo Elbow Performance Scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Postsurgical Mayo scores revealed 4 excellent results, 2 good, and 2 poor. The TTDPM in the elbows undergoing arthroplasty was still significantly higher compared with the contralateral elbow at 4.2° (15.6  ± 6.9 seconds vs. 7.2  ± 2.6 seconds; D = 3.23, P = .01) equivalent to 8.4 seconds.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Patients who have had severe joint disease requiring semiconstrained TEA have long-term proprioception deficits. A more conservative technique that maximally preserves insertions and soft tissues, might minimize upper limb proprioceptive deficit.</AbstractText>
